  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 116 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nTomorrow\n\nConsiderable showers and thunderstorms are likely tomorrow across \nall of the region. Favorable forcing aloft associated with the \nmonsoon plume will allow for locally heavy rain showers over the \nregion. Guidance indicates that we will see a much better chance of \nheavier showers over the AA fire, and a flash flood watch is in \neffect for this burn area tomorrow. The activity should push out \nonto the plains by late afternoon and into the evening hours.\n\nTemperatures over the far eastern plains should be quite hot \ntomorrow as temps will reach to around 104. Along the I-25 corridor, \nhigher clouds should move in relatively early which will temper the \ntemperatures a bit (U90s).\n\nFriday...\n\nMax temperatures should be a 2-3F cooler  than on Thursday. Plains \nwill likely be stable this day and expect most of the showers and \nstorms to remain in the mountains. Best overall showers will be over \nthe southern Sangres and San Juans. However we still could see some \nlocally heavy showers, and the wrong showers over the wrong area \ncould cause flash flooding over the burn scars. \n\nSAT-SUN-MON....\n\nHOT. Widespread 100+ readings over the plains with mid/upper 90s \nover the remainder of the plains, San Luis valley will likely see \ntemps around 90F. The mid level heat dome aloft will be over the \nregion so winds will be light. Moisture will be \"stuck\" under this \nhigh pressure aloft and there will be storms over the mtns, and if \nthey develop, they will not move much, so flash flooding will be a \nconcern over the high country.\n\nLooking at the statistical guidance, it does show a SLOW cooling \ntrend through next week. My confidence in this happening is not too \nhigh as this trend has been shown in the past and it has not \noccurred. However, we can always hope for the best. \\/Hodanish",
